Vulnerability Title
Insufficient Rate Limiting on Account Group Creation API
Vulnerability Description
It was observed that the application allows multiple account groups to be created by sending repeated requests to the account group creation API through Burp Suite Intruder. The server successfully processes these requests and creates numerous groups without an effective limit on repeated resource creation. This may allow an authenticated user to create an excessive number of account groups.
Impact
An attacker with a valid authenticated session could automate repeated group creation requests, resulting in excessive account group records, cluttered account management interfaces, increased backend resource consumption, and degraded application usability. If the behavior affects shared resources or lacks appropriate quotas, it could also contribute to resource exhaustion.
Mitigation
- Implement server-side rate limiting and throttling on the account group creation endpoint.
- Enforce a reasonable maximum number of account groups per user.
- Validate group creation requests and apply appropriate business rules.
- Consider duplicate-name validation and idempotency controls where required.
- Monitor repeated creation attempts and log unusual activity.
- Return HTTP 429 Too Many Requests when request limits are exceeded.
CVSS 3.1
Suggested severity: Medium — 6.5 (provisional)
Vector string: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:L
This score assumes an authenticated user can create excessive groups, causing a meaningful impact on data integrity and availability. Reassess the score if testing confirms only harmless duplicate records with no measurable operational impact.
Proof of Concept (PoC)
Step 1: Log in to the application using an authorized UAT account and navigate to Settings → Account → Account Group. Create a test group and intercept the corresponding API request in Burp Suite.
Step 2: Send the captured group creation request to Burp Suite Intruder. Configure a small number of repeated requests using unique test group names, then execute the test within the approved UAT scope.
Step 3: Observe that multiple requests return HTTP 200 OK and the responses contain different group IDs, indicating that multiple groups were created.
Step 4: Return to the account group settings page and refresh the interface. Verify that the newly created groups are persisted and visible in the group list.
Step 5: Capture the Intruder results, response bodies, generated group IDs, and refreshed UI showing the multiple groups as evidence of insufficient controls on repeated resource creation.
