Vulnerability Title
Insufficient Server-Side Input Validation Allows Special Characters in Watchlist Names
Vulnerability Description
It was observed that the application prevents users from entering certain special characters in the watchlist name through the user interface. However, by intercepting and modifying the corresponding API request in Burp Suite, the input validation can be bypassed, allowing watchlist names containing special characters to be submitted and saved successfully. This indicates that the input restrictions are not consistently enforced on the server side.
Impact
An authenticated user may be able to store watchlist names containing unexpected or potentially dangerous characters. Depending on how these values are processed and displayed by the application, this could cause data-integrity issues, unexpected application behavior, or potential injection vulnerabilities in downstream functionality such as watchlist exports. Further impact requires separate validation; accepting special characters alone does not establish XSS or command injection.
Mitigation
- Enforce input validation on the server side, independently of client-side validation.
- Define an explicit allowlist of permitted characters for watchlist names.
- Apply consistent validation to all API endpoints that create or update watchlists.
- Use context-appropriate output encoding wherever watchlist names are displayed.
- Validate the input length and reject malformed or unsupported values with an appropriate error response.
CVSS 3.1
Suggested score: 4.3 — Medium (provisional)
Vector string: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N
Scoring assumption: This score assumes an authenticated user can persist unexpected characters, causing a limited integrity impact. If no security-relevant impact can be demonstrated beyond bypassing a UI restriction, reassess whether this warrants a vulnerability finding.
Proof of Concept (PoC)
Step 1: Log in to the application, navigate to the Watch List functionality, and attempt to enter a watchlist name containing special characters. Observe that the UI displays the validation message: “Watch List name contains invalid characters.”
Step 2: Intercept the watchlist creation or update request using Burp Suite and identify the watchlistName parameter in the JSON body.
Step 3: Modify the watchlistName value to include the special characters that the UI normally rejects, using a harmless test string such as:
{
  "watchlistName": "VAPT_test-01"
}


Step 4: Forward the modified request and observe that the server returns a successful response, indicating that the modified value was accepted.
Step 5: Refresh the Watch List interface and verify that the watchlist name containing the previously restricted characters is displayed or persisted.
Step 6: Capture the UI validation message, modified request, successful response, and post-refresh result as evidence.
