Vulnerability Title
Insufficient Duplicate Alert Validation Leading to Duplicate Alert Generation
Vulnerability Description
It was observed that the application allows multiple identical stock price alert creation requests for the same stock symbol, price threshold, metric, and direction to be processed successfully. By intercepting the alert creation request in Burp Suite and resending it multiple times, multiple alerts are created for the same condition. When the stock price reaches the configured threshold, the application generates multiple notifications for the same alert condition instead of preventing duplicate alerts.
Impact
- Notification flooding: Users may receive repeated notifications for a single stock price event.
- Degraded user experience: Duplicate alerts make it difficult to distinguish unique trading conditions from repeated notifications.
- Resource consumption: Storing and processing redundant alerts can increase database usage and alert-processing workload.
- Operational impact: Large volumes of duplicate alerts may affect notification processing and application performance.
Mitigation
- Implement server-side duplicate detection using a normalized combination of user ID, stock symbol/instrument ID, metric, direction, threshold value, and alert status.
- Enforce an appropriate database uniqueness constraint or idempotency mechanism for duplicate alert creation requests.
- Ensure repeated requests with the same idempotency key return the existing alert instead of creating a new record.
- Apply rate limiting to alert creation endpoints to reduce automated request bursts.
- Verify that the notification service deduplicates repeated events for the same alert condition where appropriate.
CVSS 3.1
Suggested severity: Medium — provisional
CVSS score: 4.3
Vector string: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N
This assumes an authenticated user can create redundant alert records that affect application data integrity. Reassess the score if the impact is limited to notification inconvenience without meaningful integrity consequences.
Proof of Concept (PoC)
Step 1: Log in to the application, open the Alerts feature, and create one stock price alert for a specific symbol, metric, direction, and threshold.
Step 2: Intercept the alert creation request using Burp Suite and record the request parameters and resulting alert ID.
Step 3: Resend the same request multiple times using Burp Suite Intruder, keeping the stock symbol, threshold, metric, direction, and user context unchanged.
Step 4: Observe that the server returns successful responses and generates distinct alert IDs for repeated requests.
Step 5: Open the Alerts interface and verify that multiple alerts exist with the same configuration.
Step 6: When the configured price condition is met, observe that multiple notifications are generated for the same stock price event.
