Vulnerability Title
Overly Permissive Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) Configuration for Subdomains
Vulnerability Description
It was observed that the chart data API accepts a request originating from https://attacker.etrade.com and returns a successful response with the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header reflecting that origin. The response also includes Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true, indicating that cross-origin requests may be permitted with credentials. If an attacker-controlled or compromised subdomain is trusted by the application, a malicious website hosted on that subdomain could potentially access sensitive chart data belonging to an authenticated user.
Impact
- Unauthorized cross-origin access to authenticated chart data, if the browser sends valid session credentials.
- Potential exposure of sensitive trading information, including historical prices, volume, and volatility data.
- Increased risk if an attacker can control content on a trusted subdomain or exploit a subdomain takeover.
Mitigation
- Allow only explicitly approved, trusted origins rather than broadly trusting subdomains.
- Avoid dynamically reflecting the Origin header without strict server-side allowlist validation.
- Do not combine credentialed CORS access with untrusted or attacker-controlled origins.
- Review all subdomains trusted by the API and remove unnecessary entries.
- Validate the configuration using an actual browser-based proof of concept with an authenticated session.
CVSS 3.1
Suggested severity: Medium — provisional
Score: 4.3
Vector: CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N
This score assumes an attacker can induce a victim to visit a malicious page and read limited sensitive data. Recalculate it based on the actual exploitability and sensitivity of the exposed data.
Proof of Concept (PoC)
Step 1: Send an authenticated request to the chart data endpoint in Burp Suite, modifying the Origin header to https://attacker.etrade.com.
Step 2: Observe whether the response returns Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://attacker.etrade.com together with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true.
Step 3: Verify whether the specified origin is genuinely untrusted or attacker-controlled and whether a browser can read the response using the victim's authenticated session.
Step 4: Confirm whether the returned chart data contains information that should not be accessible to that origin.
